netic culprit is a g4 c2 nucleotide  repeat expansion in the intron of a gene  called c9 or f72 and this accounts for  about 40 to familial and 10  of sporadic als cases  and additional genetic contributions  have been identified through whole  genome sequencing studies  and whole genome association studies and  exome sequencing studies  revealing additional genetic culprits  and disease um often these are rare  variants that are identified in a  handful of of individuals or families or  in the case of gywa studies point to  loci  that are now being uh examined in more  detail to identify the causative variant  at these regions  but  as we're approaching 2020 and and and  beyond we're at this exciting time where  we have these new technologies available  to us where we can identify  novel risk factors and in particular  through single cell sequencing and long  read sequencing we can identify some of  these regions that really  could help explain some of the missing  credibility in als  and so  to to kind of speak about our work we  started out with one of the families  that initially had a fox variant and  we've taken a look at this family as  well and performed some genome-wise  streams to try to find if there was an  additional genetic modifier for this  region  and as you can see some of the  individuals  that are  white indicating they haven't developed  disease yet  i still have the variant  indicating that perhaps there's another  second hit or another genetic modifier  in this region  so from kind of old-school linkage  studies we'd actually mapped  this second region in this family to a  region on chromosome 18 used in sarcomas  on chromosome 16 and in this eight  chromosome 18 region all affected family  members share a four megabase segment of  dna  and the genome-wide significance of this  linkage was quite significant so we took  a look within this region to see whether  there were other risk factors that could  could help explain some of the missing  additional genetic risk factors in this  family  and uh this is just to kind of point out  uh by using both this chromosome 18  locus and the plus variant we could  start to explain some of the some of the  individuals that uh  had developed als but hadn't um  that are shaded in black um but did not  perhaps have a phosphate  and this uh this kind of like  combination is best exemplified by these  four siblings here  uh where you can see uh they have all  four possible genotypes either the fuss  variant  neither variant the chromosome 18 locus  or both and it was only the ones with  both that had that developed disease  and so in our search we came across this  human specific  uh tandem repeat expansion that each  copy of the repeat is 69 base pairs  and so  this is in the last intron of a gene  called wdr7  and each copy of the repeat actually  folds into this really interesting uh  hairpin-like structure where there's  regions of self-complementarity and then  a loop region and then  and then a gap and then another hairpin  the reference genome has about six  copies of copies of this repeat but  actually individuals  in that family had over uh over 30  copies of this repeat  again when this repeat is inserted into  rna folding algorithms it reveals this  really unique hairpin structure  of the centronic sequence  so the gene wdr7 itself encodes a  protein that localizes to synaptic  vessels uh vesicles involved in the  control of calcium-dependent exocytosis  of neurotransmitters  as well as acidification of endosomal  vesicles so all of these are relevant to  als etiology so we wanted to take a look  further  at  wdr7  function and kind of the evolution of  this repeat expansion  what really surprised us was that this  is a this is almost exclusively a human  specific repeat uh it's not present in  any lower mammals um  including uh including lemurs and and  other  non-primates  old world monkeys  starting with the squirrel monkey start  to  start to have about a 4