quence if there's a color it means  there's a base per change and there's  six positions where there are base pair  changes  throughout the repeat  and so what we were what we did was we  converted this to a single  color for each of the most common the 16  most common uh repeat sequences that we  observed and you can see each one has a  different combination of these internal  uh six uh variants that are present  when we did this we found that there  wasn't actually a difference between  each of the internal sequences between  als cases and controls but we could  still use this information to try to  design a pattern and convert some of  this information to a kind of a  color-coded map  so again from each individual we took we  identified the start of the repeat we  aligned this placed each repeat into a  separate row  converted this actually to a single  letter code to avoid some of the  complexities of this information and  converted that code to a color and the  reason why we chose these single letter  codes is that we could actually use  amino acid clustering alignment programs  to align some of the repeats to identify  patterns in their structure  and then the colors helped us visualize  things  rather than a series of letters together  on the slide we can kind of like  immediately kind of your eyes drawn to  some of the patterns in this sequence  and so this is a complex plot that we  put together but what you can see here  is that the repeat length is here on the  on the y-axis and individual alleles are  on the x-axis and starting on the left  you have about eight or so allele uh  individuals with you know um  five two three four and four copies of  the wdr7 repeats for each of them  we continue to do this iteratively um  and we when we were able to put it  together we saw this complex pattern of  uh of the repeats but we noticed  something and we were always kind of  sequencing this from the five prime to  three prime direction but in fact the  three prime