 ones we would color in black and these  ones would be up here these ones would  appear right at the five time end of the  repeat and then not only would they  appear once they would actually if they  if they appeared a second time like this  yellow black segment they actually  expanded in combination with that  previous repeat unit again suggesting  there's something unique about uh about  this 138 or so nucleotides of a  that was important for this expansion  event  the initial sequencing run that we had  performed was actually  selected for individuals with 20 repeats  or more because we were really concerned  about being able to amplify some of that  internal sequence with the really large  repeats once we started seeing those  patterns we then went and repeated the  tactile long read sequencing run with a  second cohort of individuals and the  second cohort um had  additional populations both with a.d and  control but also individuals from the  thousand genomes population uh allowing  for kind of a more diverse cohort of  individuals uh  looking at repeat extensions  what we noticed because we didn't and in  this case we didn't size select so we  were noticing several alleles on the  left that were of the shorter nature but  we also saw that exact same pattern that  we saw before where you had these  clusters of the same kind of  segments of repeats indicating that  the alignment that we put together was  reproducible  and then using some of these thousand  genomes population  samples from worldwide populations we  could start to see patterns emerging  from some of the groups so for instance  it locks here um samples from africa on  the left and samples that were outside  of africa that had the kind of the same  uh original base structure and then  halfway through there's this divergence  point between pink and purple alleles  and african samples maintain their  trajectory from there on in out  and non-africans have a different um  alleles that are added  uh suggesting that perhaps there was a  an evolutionary time point when this uh  this breakpoint occurred and we saw the  same phenomenon in uh repeat expansions  that were specific to han chinese um  individuals where they had  expanded using their own combination of  these internal repeat sequences  so we think this is a fascinating kind  of  tool that can be used to kind of track  evolutionary expansion of  some of these tandem repeats across  different populations  from a multi-generational standpoint  within one pedigree we can use the  information to again  track segregation from parents to  children  and because we can combine this with the  internal sequence information we can  actually find individuals with similar  length  alleles but actually different internal  sequence within them  and then another cool thing that we  could do was then because we had all  this information that we gathered from  longread sequencing we could go back to  short read sequencing and whole genome  sequencing to try to determine and  predict uh what alleles were present in  certain populations and in particular we  were interested in ancient populations  like denisovans and neanderthals  um and so as you can see here this is an  igb plot  where the top panel  is is the alignment of short reads to  the wdr7 intron and in the middle is the  region with the vntr and you can see  there's a very strong pile up a short  reef at that vmtr itself uh suggesting  that it's much larger than the reference  gene human genome which has six copies  and conversely neanderthals uh have more  reads at the surrounding genomic region  but a paucity of reads at the vntr  itself suggesting that perhaps it is a  shorter  repeat than than in the human presence  genome and in fact we can actually go  and identify what  because of our with with that internal  sequence what the actual repeat units  are so we can see that there are four  different repeat units present in the  neanderthals and so they either have a  combination of one and three copies or  two and two copies of the repeat and  then denise evans we can map the number  of repeats  and the number of counts of repeat copy  numbers and actually use this  information to try to  reconstruct potentially their  their ancestral  allele at wdrs at the wdr7 locus  and  beyond this one repeat we were really  interested you know can we generalize  this information across the um  you know the 55 000 variable number  tandem repeats in the human genome and  variable number meaning uh repeats with  the  internal sequence  composition seven nucleotides or greater  and so for this we took advantage of a  couple of publications from the uh from  the group of evan eichler um  including this one here that maps uh  variable number 10 and retreats across  the genome and we can see there's a lot  of them in green that are uh that  cluster around telomeres or sub-temp  telomeric regions but we also had enough  within genes and around genes that we  could uh extract the information and  take a look  at whether they expanded in a manner  similar to the wdr7 repeat  and our selection criteria were  ultimately around 400 repeats that are  human-specific with a single site in the  genome and a repeat unit 25 nucleotides  or greater  and what we were able to do was take  advantage of  15  individuals again from the thousand  genomes population sample set that had  undergone long read phase sequencing  and these individuals had  aligned here on the y-axis had various  copies of some of the repeats that we  were taking a look at  and so each kind of uh  each of each row here represents a  different individual and they're  combinations of repeat lengths  in this case for a 41 nucleotide uh  repeat unit in a in the intron of a gene  called nwd2  and as you can see when we kind of like  took a look at some of the um the repeat  structure in the organization this is  kind of what we were expecting to see is  that again similar to wdr7 we  color