ditional genetic risk factors in this  family  and uh this is just to kind of point out  uh by using both this chromosome 18  locus and the plus variant we could  start to explain some of the some of the  individuals that uh  had developed als but hadn't um  that are shaded in black um but did not  perhaps have a phosphate  and this uh this kind of like  combination is best exemplified by these  four siblings here  uh where you can see uh they have all  four possible genotypes either the fuss  variant  neither variant the chromosome 18 locus  or both and it was only the ones with  both that had that developed disease  and so in our search we came across this  human specific  uh tandem repeat expansion that each  copy of the repeat is 69 base pairs  and so  this is in the last intron of a gene  called wdr7  and each copy of the repeat actually  folds into this really interesting uh  hairpin-like structure where there's  regions of self-complementarity and then  a loop region and then  and then a gap and then another hairpin  the reference genome has about six  copies of copies of this repeat but  actually individuals  in that family had over uh over 30  copies of this repeat  again when this repeat is inserted into  rna folding algorithms it reveals this  really unique hairpin structure  of the centronic sequence  so the gene wdr7 itself encodes a  protein that localizes to synaptic  vessels uh vesicles involved in the  control of calcium-dependent exocytosis  of neurotransmitters  as well as acidification of endosomal  vesicles so all of these are relevant to  als etiology so we wanted to take a look  further  at  wdr7  function and kind of the evolution of  this repeat expansion  what really surprised us was that this  is a this is almost exclusively a human  specific repeat uh it's not present in  any lower mammals um  including uh including lemurs and and  other  non-primates  old world monkeys  starting with the squirrel monkey start  to  start to have about a 47 base pair  region of the repeat so it's about  two-thirds of the repeat  start to appear in these genomes  by the time you get by the time you get  to old world monkeys here starting with  the orangutan you actually jump to one  and a half copies of the repeat and by  the time you get to humans you have six  and a half copies up to 33 copies  uh in this uh in this als family so we  could track the  progression and the expansion of the  three feet uh throughout primate  evolution  um  but we were also interested kind of  beyond this uh beyond this role  you know what uh  is this a risk factor  in als beyond just the family  essentially and so to do this we were  curious uh to take a look at individuals  with sporadic als the large cohorts  and try to identify the repeat size to  see if we could find additional uh  individuals with high repeat copy member  and we're really fortunate in that we  were able to actually pcr amplify this  repeat we were able to design on the  left here in orange primers either at  the beginning or at the end of the  repeat  and we could actually then sequence  through  various copies of the repeat and shown  on the left is four copies with the  repeat  but on the right is a is a gel  uh of a pcr and you can see um each of  the rows is a different individual with  uh often two alleles of different  lengths  so we were able to actually  use this information to  pcr amplify and  run on the gel hundreds of als cases and  controls from the cory lcell repository  of bank dna samples and when we did this  we actually found an enrichment  of repeats that were 30 or more in  length in individuals with als  relative to controls uh we found cases  where there perhaps was like a  premutation uh just below that threshold  and we also found one individual at the  far right in orange with 86 copies of  the wdr7 repeat this is a female with a  without a family history of als who died  at the age of 72.  so combining all of these samples and  just measuring the largest or the  longest repeat  in each we actually could see that there  was a shift  to longer average repeat in als samples  to 17.7 copies versus 15.2 copies and  controls  and  what we were able to do was then use  this information to uh  and confirm this information using two  additional populations and we use full  genome sequencing um and read up to  estimate uh copy number uh in two  cohorts one from answer als and another  in samples from quebec canada  but so in our mind even though there's  control individuals have uh high numbers  of our high and repeat copies as well  there's a shift there's this kind of  modifying effect in the context of als  the second thing we wanted to do and  this relates to the  spinal fca uh pentanucleotide repeat  that i was showing earlier was to  determine whether there was any internal  repeat sequence that was different in  als so to do this we took those same  primers that we used to amplify wdr7 but  we added barcodes to the five prime and  three prime end and commentorial use of  those barcodes allowed us to actually  pcr amplify  144 individuals or 288 alleles in one  lane of tactile long lead sequencing  and we had a problem though  and the problem was that  how do we then take all that information  and encapsulate it and be able to align  it to identify if there was enrichment  and and one of the examples here is uh  is that 86 copies of the repeat where  you you see have  over and over again that repeat  expansion through the genetic sequence  and we needed to find a way to kind of  align that along with all some of the  other als individuals and controls that  we had  that underwent long green sequencing  so we used a couple of tricks  and the first thing that we wanted to do  was to look at the potential internal  sequence at each repeat unit so again  the wdr7 repeat is 69 nucleotides in  length and of those 69 nucleotides in  color you can see if a dot if there's a  dot it's the same as the consensus  sequence if there's a color it means  there's a base per change and there's  six positions where there are base pair  changes  throughout the repeat  and so what we were what we did was we  converted this to a single  color for each of the most common the 16  most common uh repeat sequences that we  observed and you can see each one has a  different combination of these internal  uh six uh variants that are present  when we did this we found that there  wasn't actually a difference between  each of the internal sequences between  als cases and controls but we could  still use this information to try to  design a pattern and convert some of  this information to a kind of a  color-coded map  so again from each individual we took we  identified the start of the repeat we  aligned this placed each repeat into a  separate row  converted this actually to a single  letter code to avoid some of the  complexities of this information and  converted that code to a color and the  reason why we chose these single letter  codes is that we could actually use  amino acid clustering alignment programs  to align some of the repeats to identify  patterns in their structure  and then the colors helped us visualize  things  rather than a series of letters together  on the slide we can kind of like  immediately kind of your eyes drawn to  some of the patterns in this sequence  and so this is a complex plot that we  put together but what you can see here  is that the repeat length is here on the  on the y-axis and individual alleles are  on the x-axis and starting on the left  you have about eight or so allele uh  individuals with you know um  five two three four and four copies of  the wdr7 repeats for each of them  we continue to do this iteratively um  and we when we were able to put it  together we saw this complex pattern of  uh of the repeats but we noticed  something and we were always kind of  sequencing this from the five prime to  three prime direction but in fact the  three prime uh region of the repeat had  a much more similar much more similarity  in terms of the colors that were present  and so when we realigned these on the  three prime end we started seeing these  amazing patterns and clusters of groups  of individuals it each had the same  a similar combination of the of the of  the wdr7 repeat and these were often  very much fixed at the  three prime end but then started to  diverge uh at the five prime end  and there were several kind of different  uh original clusters you can see ones  with a number of dark blue reeds numbers  with yellows and  purples and  and then dark red  and as every time we kind of looked at  this repeat we learned we learned more  information we started noticing patterns  that were emerging from this data one of  the first things we noticed was there  was this seemed to be this kind of  biphasic kind of um introduction in the  repeat so the repeats weren't showing up  one at a time but often in groups of two  and you can see that uh especially in  that in the middle of this repeat where  there is this dark red and light purple  uh clusters of repeats and so when we  analyze these iteratively across all of  the uh 288 alleles from 144 individuals  we saw this uh kind of periodicity of  the repeats where you're more likely to  see  uh  repeats the same repeat  two repeat units from each other four  repeat units six eight and so on than  you were to see the repeat unit  immediately adjacent to itself so  there's there's something about the two  repeat units together uh that  precipitates some of this expansion  we could see large duplication events in  some uh  in some of the cases you can see these  this group of about six repeat units  that were  that were copied several times into the  same in the same individual  and we can also see that again speaking  to the three point to five prime  directionality of the repeat uh whenever  we saw a new repeat unit and one that  wasn't part of that original 16 um these  ones we would color in black and these  ones would be up here these ones would  appear right at the five time end of the  repeat and then not only would they  appear once they would actually if they  if they appeared a second time like this  yellow black segment they actually  expanded in combination with that  previous repeat unit again suggesting  there's something unique about uh about  this 138 or so nucleotides of a  that was important for this expansion  event  the initial sequencing run that we had  performed was actually  selected for individuals with 20 repeats  or more because we were really concerned  about being able to amplify some of that  internal sequence with the really large  repeats once we started seeing those  patterns we then went and repeated the  tactile long read sequencing run with a  second cohort of individuals and the  second cohort um had  additional populations both with a.d and  control but also individuals from the  thousand genomes population uh allowing  for kind of a more diverse cohort of  individuals uh  looking at repeat extensions  what we noticed because we didn't and in  this case we didn't size select so we  were noticing several alleles on the  left that were of the shorter nature but  we also saw that exact same pattern that  we saw before where you had these  clusters of the same kind of  segments of repeats indicating that  the alignment that we put together was  reproducible  and then using some of these thousand  genomes population  samples from worldwide populations we  could start to see patterns emerging  from some of the groups so for instance  it locks here um samples from africa on  the left and samples that were outside  of africa that had the kind of the same  uh original base structure and then  halfway through there's this divergence  point between pink and purple alleles  and african samples maintain their  trajectory from there on in out  and non-africans have a different um  alleles that are added  uh suggesting that perhaps there was a  an evolutionary time point when this uh  this breakpoint occurred and we saw the  same phenomenon in uh repeat expansions  that were specific to han chinese um  individuals where they had  expanded using their own combination of  these internal repeat sequences  so we think this is a fascinating kind  of  tool that can be used to kind of track  evolutionary expansion of  some of these tandem repeats across  different populations  from a multi-generational standpoint  within one pedigree we can use the  information to again  track segregation from parents to  children  and because we can combine this with the  internal sequence information we can  actually find individuals with similar  length  alleles but actually different internal  sequence within them  and then another cool thing that we  could do was then because we had all  this information that we gathered from  longread sequencing we could go back to  short read sequencing and whole genome  sequencing to try to determine and  predict uh what alleles were present in  certain populations and in particular we  were interested in ancient populations  like denisovans and neanderthals  um and so as you can see here this is an  igb plot  where the top panel  is is the alignment of short reads to  the wdr7 intron and in the middle is the  region with the vntr and you can see  there's a very strong pile up a short  reef at that vmtr itself uh suggesting  that it's much larger than the reference  gene human genome which has six copies  and conversely neanderthals uh have more  reads at the surrounding genomic region  but a paucity of reads at the vntr  itself suggesting that perhaps it is a  shorter  repeat than than in the human presence  genome and in fact we can actually go  and identify what  because of our with with that internal  sequence what the actual repeat units  are so we can see that there are four  different repeat units present in the  neanderthals and so they either have a  combination of one and three copies or  two and two copies of the repeat and  then denise evans we can map the number  of repeats  and the number of counts of repeat copy  numbers and actually use this  information to try to  reconstruct potentially their  their ancestral  allele at wdrs at the wdr7 locus  and  beyond this one repeat we were really  interested you know can we generalize  this information across the um  you know the 55 000 variable number  tandem repeats in the human genome and  variable number meaning uh repeats with  the  internal sequence  composition seven nucleotides or greater  and so for this we took advantage of a  couple of publications from the uh from  the group of evan eichler um  including this one here that maps uh  variable number 10 and retreats across  the genome and we can see there's a lot  of them in green that are uh that  cluster around telomeres or sub-temp  telomeric regions but we also had enough  within genes and around genes that we  could uh extract the information and  take a look  at whether they expanded in a manner  similar to the wdr7 repeat  and our selection criteria were  ultimately around 400 repeats that are  human-specific with a single site in the  genome and a repeat unit 25 nucleotides  or greater  and what we were able to do was take  advantage of  15  individuals again from the thousand  genomes population sample set that had  undergone long read phase sequencing  and these individuals had  aligned here on the y-axis had various  copies of some of the repeats that we  were taking a look at  and so each kind of uh  each of each row here represents a  different individual and they're  combinations of repeat lengths  in this case for a 41 nucleotide uh  repeat unit in a in the intron of a gene  called nwd2  and as you can see when we kind of like  took a look at some of the um the repeat  structure in the organization this is  kind of what we were expecting to see is  that again similar to wdr7 we  color