e were the three kind of main  categories of tandem repeats we we  observed when looking at long-range  sequencing  um but we also found some really  interesting outliers and and unique kind  of nuggets in terms of uh their their  properties and in this case this is a  it's a 33 base pair repeat unit in the  three prime utr of a gene called bps53  and it has three separate segments  within its uh internal tendon repeat so  the first segment is fixed in length but  variable at each comp at each copy of  the repeat so each copy of the repeat  had a different internal sequence  and then you had a second segment where  there was mostly a pure sequence but  some variability in length  apart from these few individuals with a  kind of a a darker blue color  and then you have this uh really  interesting third segment um where there  were  combinations of three repeat units  together that were expanding and and  quite a bit of diversity in terms of the  repeat length  so that segment three is the one that  seems to be actively expanding and one  that we're really interested in  determining you know um as as you know  as we identify more individuals you know  what accounts for some of this  variability and length and why is it  expanding again in combinations of three  repeat units or about 100  base pairs  versus the the wd or 7 repeats it seems  to be expanding in two repeat copies  and we could find again that we could  really kind of use this information to  then narrow down exactly the components  involved with the repeat expansions um  as we kind of sequence as we analyzed  more and more individuals using long  read sequencing so again this is a this  is a repeat in the gene called slp 22a1  where you have  a bit more than 40 copies of that repeat  in some individuals  and you can see here there are gaps so  these gaps in white represent uh kind of  deletion events that are that are  present uh that are  or the deletions that are present in  certain individuals  we also